{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1565181-integrity","id":1565181,"title":"Integrity:","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-11-08T10:17:38-08:00","id":1565181,"title":"Integrity:","content":"Help me understand just how integrity works. The rules in CoD are really lax, and they don't explain much, but, so, is like you got your integrity-dots, then, something happens, and you have to roll to resist (Resolve + Composer, or something to that effect), losing your Integrity-dots, like witnessing the supernatural (does that apply only to major splats or, something minor, like ghosts or magic from Second-Sight?), or an action that compromises your virtue(s), which, is kinda vague, but, you got plus or minus modifiers via a chart on page, 74 (Breaking-point mods), and you go from there?<br /> <br /> Also, how does Conditions and Aspirations come into play? <br /> <br /> Do you have examples of gameplay?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"740"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-11-08T13:57:41-08:00","id":1565185,"title":"","content":"Your Integrity is a measure of how firm your self-concept is, informed by things like your sense of right and wrong (both personally and culturally) and your understanding of the way the world works (both in an actual literal sense and in terms of the way the society you inhabit and grew up in understands reality). Being the victim or perpetrator of violence or trauma is an aberrant circumstance for most people, and thus it threatens their Integrity. Witnessing violations of the conventionally-understood rules of the world do likewise, especially if they involve violence or trauma \u2014 however compelling the spectacle may be, the bit of your metaphysical anatomy that holds your soul recognizes that <i>something is wrong</i>. Your Integrity is in this way a measure of how faithfully you abide by your own perspective on the way things are supposed to be; magic is taboo because it's skirting the rules at best and breaking the agreement entirely at worst.<br /> <br /> The roll to try to retain your Integrity uses the same Attributes as determine the reserves of your Willpower (itself a means of asserting your selfhood in the world) as a reflection of how resilient your self-concept is. Regular success or failure are only differentiated by whether you actually lose a dot of Integrity \u2014 conventionally, the act or exposure makes you feel bad or unsettled in a way that incentivizes some sort of response (even if it's the perverse impulse to associate yourself more with the supernatural, which Geist supplements by suggesting Obsession as an alternate Condition for breaking points caused by supernatural objects), and the combination of the risk to your not-quite-mental-health and the consequences of the resulting Condition is meant to suggest that most people do not deliberately repeatedly expose themselves to circumstances that disagree with their morals and metaphysical understanding, though the unpredictability of how people react to the supernatural in general is itself an incentive for intelligent monsters to keep their activities under wraps.<br /> <br /> The effects of dramatic failure are similarly a higher degree of warning for the spiritually frail (whether from low Willpower, low Integrity, or both) by the greater and more durable consequences of those breaking points (and the greater risk of suffering them). Exceptional success does the opposite \u2014 no Condition, you keep your Integrity, and you even recover a Willpower point and get a Beat \u2014 and is in some ways an incentive for characters with high Integrity and Willpower to pursue benign manifestations of the supernatural and risk their code and cultural fidelity in pursuit of their Virtue and/or the protection of their loved ones \u2014 the fact that the mechanical requirements for a character to trigger an Awakening in play include exceptional success on a breaking point in confrontation with the Lie, and that mages deprived of their souls lose Gnosis whenever they lose Willpower dots, are not coincidental.<br /> <br /> Hunter's twist on Integrity, as well as Demon's slanted equivalent trait Cover, both diverge from conventional Integrity breaking points by changing the roll results, albeit in opposite directions:<br /> <br /> Hunters have taken up the Vigil and their Code affects their breaking points both by adopting a format that allows them to deaden themselves to risk for certain experiences (further mitigated by the ability to benefit from Touchstones) and by making it so that on a regular success they <i>don't respond to trauma like regular people do</i> \u2014 a hunter who comes out the other side of a breaking point with their Integrity unchanged gains a Condition that offers a benefit to go with its temporary drawbacks but gains more ingrained Conditions on an exceptional success, while the consequences of dramatic failure are less long-lasting than normal but more likely to cause problems.<br /> <br /> Demons, meanwhile, react like normal people on breaking point equivalents (including those that come from replacing the soul of someone whose breaking points are now compromises for the Cover it was used to make), with exceptional success only differing from Integrity by what it signifies and the ability to take a different type of Beat; failure and dramatic failure are how the demon's true nature comes out in a way that invites pursuit.<br /> <br /> Aspirations don't have any direct mechanical impact on breaking point rolls for Integrity, though mages have Obsessions that resemble long-term Aspirations related to the supernatural that make it more likely they will lose Wisdom if they commit Acts of Hubris in their pursuit (and, paradoxically, in order for them to raise their Wisdom they need to dedicate an Obsession and a story of pursuing it to do so), and as character-steering mechanisms an Aspiration may lead a character into situations where they may suffer a breaking point. Conditions may have an impact on breaking point rolls (whether directly or by things like giving a character an extra Vice), but that's as a function of the flexibility of Conditions as a game mechanic rather than anything intrinsic to breaking points.<br /> <br /> Some of this is kind of self-explanatory from the description in the book, so I'm not sure what elements aren't clicking \u2014 the roll, roll results, and common modifiers are as-printed.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-11-08T14:15:06-08:00","id":1565186,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Integrity is your character\u2019s mental stability, their ability to relate to people, their own moral worth, or their sanity. When they experience something traumatizing or that shakes their sense of reality, you roll Resolve + Composure as they try to process it.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Exceptional success means they power through, regain a Willpower and take a beat. Success means they are processing the shock and gain a condition, but they will be mostly fine. Failure means they are not ok, they lose Integrity, gain a condition and the experience damages their mental wellness. A dramatic failure means the trauma runs deep and leaves a mental scar, so they not only lose Integrity, but gain a persistent condition. Regardless of the result, making a Breaking Point roll grants a Beat.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Their current Integrity level and circumstances modify the roll.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">An example was that the character Trish (an Atariya) sneaked past some bank robbering cultists in hannya masks, in order to steal the biwa they were using to knock out the bank's power. She got her entire health box full of lethal, escaped them, was able to run into the open vault and was trying to destroy the biwa. A cultist ran after her and she saw her best friend run in and dismember the cultist with a katana, before passing out from her wounds.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">She had to roll for two breaking points, one for being shot up, as she is not used to combat, and the second for seeing her friend mangle someone. When she woke up, she rolled both. We consulted the chart to modify each based on her current Integrity, the fact she was acting on both her Virtue and Vice, etc. She passed both rolls, but she became Shaken (of her friend's brutality) and Spooked (of the cultists).</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">She woke up in an Arrows dojo (&quot;I'm in some samurai cult, was she in it ? Who is she really ?&quot;), completely healed despite her clothes being bloodied and full of bullet wounds. Her friend was apologetic for what she did, but was having a hard time explaining mages to her. Trish, still Spooked and not getting the answers her panic demanded, ran towards a police booth, causing a chase. It drew public attention, got an officer to be called in and ended up in social media because she was crying out for help. So even though they did manage to reconcile and went to talk in a family diner, they had to deal with the officer coming in to check in on her, plus some temporary Fame from the public fallout. As things wound down, she resolved Spooked and took a beat.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">She is still terrified of the cultists, when that fear causes her to fail a roll as her player chooses to resolve Shaken, she will take another Beat. And that is how those breaking points were reflected in roleplay.<br /> <br /> Hope that proves helpful.</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>New experiences are the font of creativity, when seeking inspiration, break your routine.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1278855-the-agathos-kai-sophos-a-acanthus-legacy-of-strategists\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#1155CC\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Agathos Kai Sophos</span></span></a><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, an Acanthus Legacy of strategists (Mind/Time)</span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1360568-the-szary-stra%C5%BCnik-an-obrimos-legacy-of-scholars-of-the-glyphs-of-fate\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#1155CC\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Szary Stra\u017cnik</span></span></a><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, an Obrimos Legacy whose invisible hands guide through the Glyphs of Fate (Fate/Prime)</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24280-kaiserafini","name":"KaiserAfini","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24280_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2648"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}